WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.241
A batch job holds a lock for one and a half seconds.

2
00:00:06.241 --> 00:00:11.076
An API thread needs the same lock, and it has a 200 millisecond budget.

3
00:00:11.367 --> 00:00:14.912
With synchronized, the API thread can only wait at the door.

4
00:00:14.912 --> 00:00:19.935
It can't give up after a timeout, and an interrupt doesn't reach it until it gets in.

5
00:00:20.233 --> 00:00:28.002
ReentrantLock is the same room with the same rules: one owner, a release happens-before the next lock, and it's reentrant.

6
00:00:28.002 --> 00:00:30.359
What it adds is controls on the door.

7
00:00:30.633 --> 00:00:37.872
tryLock with a timeout waits at most 200 milliseconds, then returns false, and the thread can answer busy.

8
00:00:38.167 --> 00:00:42.355
lockInterruptibly waits too, but an interrupt gets it out at once.

9
00:00:44.433 --> 00:00:47.228
Next, a buffer with room for two payments.

10
00:00:47.228 --> 00:00:54.879
A monitor has one wait set, so producers and consumers sleep in the same place, and notify can wake the wrong kind.

11
00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:59.994
A ReentrantLock can have one waiting room per reason, called a Condition.

12
00:00:59.994 --> 00:01:01.804
Consumers wait in notEmpty.

13
00:01:01.804 --> 00:01:03.547
Producers wait in notFull.

14
00:01:03.767 --> 00:01:05.080
The buffer is empty.

15
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.380
A consumer calls await on notEmpty.

16
00:01:07.380 --> 00:01:10.993
That gives back the lock, and parks it in its own room.

17
00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:13.095
A second consumer does the same.

18
00:01:13.300 --> 00:01:18.163
A producer takes the lock, adds a payment, and calls signal on notEmpty.

19
00:01:18.163 --> 00:01:19.649
Which thread wakes up?

20
00:01:22.233 --> 00:01:22.916
A consumer.

21
00:01:22.916 --> 00:01:24.962
Only consumers wait in that room.

22
00:01:24.962 --> 00:01:30.296
It goes back to the door for the lock, then checks the buffer again in its while loop.

23
00:01:31.300 --> 00:01:37.017
Last, an FX rate cache, read 5,000 times a second and refreshed every 5 seconds.

24
00:01:37.017 --> 00:01:41.233
With a plain lock, readers wait for each other for nothing.

25
00:01:41.467 --> 00:01:45.449
A ReentrantReadWriteLock lets any number of readers in together.

26
00:01:45.449 --> 00:01:50.054
The writer needs the room empty, so it waits until the last reader leaves.

27
00:01:50.054 --> 00:01:52.729
Then it writes alone, and new readers wait.

28
00:01:53.567 --> 00:01:55.678
In code, nothing lets go for you.

29
00:01:55.678 --> 00:01:59.838
Call lock just before try, and release it first thing in finally.

30
00:01:59.838 --> 00:02:02.269
Kotlin's withLock writes that for you.

31
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.924
Explicit locks keep the rules of synchronized.

32
00:02:05.924 --> 00:02:11.817
They add a door you can leave, a waiting room per condition, and a read mode many threads share.

33
00:02:12.033 --> 00:02:12.896
Quick check.

34
00:02:12.896 --> 00:02:17.496
Two consumers wait in notEmpty, and a producer waits in notFull.

35
00:02:17.496 --> 00:02:20.875
A consumer takes a payment and signals notFull.

36
00:02:20.875 --> 00:02:21.809
Who wakes up?
